/-!
# Simultaneous normal crossings subboundary charts and branch levels

Source: ILO, Travaux de Gabber, Expose VIII 4.1.2, 4.1.6--4.1.8, pp. 117--118;
Stacks Project, Tags 0BSF and 0BI9 (blueprint: lem-trf-nc-subboundary-branch-atlas).
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v

open CategoryTheory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData

/-- A reduced union of boundary components admits simultaneous affine etale charts.
Geometric points are pairs `(x, ι : κ(x) → Ω)` with `Ω` algebraically closed, in any
larger universe. A pointed lift is expressed by the residue-field compatibility equation.
An affine `W` is canonically `Spec Γ(W, ⊤)` by `W.isoSpec`. Vanishing is evaluation in
`κ(u)`. Equalities of ideal sheaf data are scheme-theoretic equations, and the support of
the ideal generated by a selected family is its closed coordinate intersection.
Source: ILO VIII 4.1.2 and 4.1.6--4.1.8; Stacks 0BSF, 0BI9
(blueprint: lem-trf-nc-subboundary-branch-atlas).

Proof:
1. Fix $\bar x$. Choose the pointed neighborhood $V$ in the explicit neighborhood hypothesis. At
its chosen point $w$, take a full parameter system with $B_V$ generated by its first $r$
factors. Restrict to an affine open around $w$ and represent all parameter germs by functions
there. This affine is Noetherian by [pqf-def-locnoeth]. The equality of the ideal of $B_V$ with
the principal product holds after shrinking: both ideals are finitely generated in the affine
Noetherian ring, and each of the finitely many containments holding after localization holds
after inverting one element outside the prime of $w$. Intersect these finitely many principal
opens. Call the resulting affine $W$ and its functions $t_1,\ldots,t_d$. The lift of $\bar x$ is
retained by [lem-weil-pbc-pointed-etale-neighbourhood-affine-shrink]. Thus $B_W=V(t_1\cdots
t_r)$ scheme-theoretically and all displayed factors vanish at $w$.
Uses: pqf-def-locnoeth, lem-weil-pbc-pointed-etale-neighbourhood-affine-shrink.
2. The pointwise parameter hypothesis holds everywhere on $W$, so
[lem-sfb-snc-boundary-parameter-criterion] gives its strict normal crossings boundary structure.
In particular its finitely many reduced irreducible components $C_a$ are regular. Apply
[lem-trf-etale-component-coordinate-identification] with $X=W$, $D=B_W$, and the identity etale
map, using the equation from Step 1. After shrinking around $w$, its components are precisely
the hyperplanes $C_i=V(t_i)$ for $1\le i\le r$; components missing $w$ have been removed. At any
other $u$ apply the same identification to the identity map and a parameter presentation of
$B_W$ at $u$. It assigns the components through $u$ to distinct parameter hyperplanes. Thus each
$t_i$ vanishing at $u$ generates the same principal ideal as its assigned parameter. By
[lem-trf-regular-local-ufd-boundary-factorization] the local ring is a domain. In this domain
the two generators differ by a unit; multiplying the corresponding parameter by that unit
retains a full parameter system. This proves the required uniform distinct-parameter condition
for the fixed functions $t_i$.
Uses: lem-sfb-snc-boundary-parameter-criterion,
lem-trf-etale-component-coordinate-identification,
lem-trf-regular-local-ufd-boundary-factorization.
3. Let $C$ be an irreducible component of $D_W$. Its generic point maps to a component-generic
point of $D$: the map $D_W\to D$ is etale by [lem-weil-pbc-etale-scheme-base-change], hence flat
by [def-weil-etale-morphism]. On affine charts its ring map is flat by
[lem-weil-pbc-flat-morphism-affine-criterion]. By
[lem-weil-pbc-spec-irreducible-components-minimal-primes], the source generic prime is minimal.
Going down [lem-sc-flat-going-down] shows that its contraction is minimal too: a strictly
smaller contracted prime would lift to a strictly smaller source prime. Hence the generic image
is generic in a component of $D$, which by hypothesis is a component of $B$. Since $C$ is
contained in $B_W$, it is contained in some $C_i$. The generic point of $C_i$ maps to the
generic point of a component of $B$, by the same flat going-down argument. Its image generalizes
that of the generic point of $C$; both are component-generic in $B$, so the images agree. By
[lem-weil-pbc-etale-fibre-discrete-separable-points], the fibre of $B_W\to B$ has discrete
topology. Two comparable points of that fibre are therefore equal. Consequently $C=C_i$. Thus
$|D_W|$ is the union of a subset of the hyperplanes $C_i$.
Uses: lem-weil-pbc-etale-scheme-base-change, def-weil-etale-morphism,
lem-weil-pbc-flat-morphism-affine-criterion,
lem-weil-pbc-spec-irreducible-components-minimal-primes, lem-sc-flat-going-down,
lem-weil-pbc-etale-fibre-discrete-separable-points.
4. Renumber the selected hyperplanes as $1,\ldots,s$. The pullback $D_W$ is reduced by
[lem-trf-etale-reduced-cartier-descent]. At $u\in W$ discard those selected $t_i$ which are
units. The remaining factors are distinct regular parameters by Step 2. They are prime in the
regular local domain by [lem-trf-regular-local-ufd-boundary-factorization]. Their product
generates a radical ideal: if the product divides $a^n$, each prime factor divides $a$, and
their pairwise nonassociate prime factors therefore have product dividing $a$. The empty product
generates the unit ideal. Hence $V(t_1\cdots t_s)$ is reduced and has exactly the support found
in Step 3. Uniqueness of reduced induced closed structure
[lem-weil-pbc-reduced-induced-closed-subscheme] gives $D_W=V(t_1\cdots t_s)$ as closed
subschemes. This proves both simultaneous equations, including $D_W=\varnothing$ when $s=0$.
Uses: lem-trf-etale-reduced-cartier-descent, lem-trf-regular-local-ufd-boundary-factorization,
lem-weil-pbc-reduced-induced-closed-subscheme.
5. For $s\ge1$, [lem-trf-nc-coordinate-max-stratum] identifies the count $\#\{j\le s:t_j(u)=0\}$
with the number of minimal primes of the reduced strict henselian local ring of $D_W$, and
proves its coordinate independence and etale invariance. To compare with every germ presentation
allowed in [def-trf-normal-crossings-branch-count], apply
[lem-trf-parameter-product-strict-local-branches] to its regular local chart ring and its
selected parameters: its length equals the number of strict-local minimal primes. For the etale
map from that chart to $X$, [lem-trf-ind-etale-strict-local-invariance] identifies the ambient
strict localizations at the same geometric point. Pulling back the defining ideal therefore
identifies the strict-local divisor quotients. Thus all presentation lengths equal our
coordinate count; their set is a singleton and its infimum is that count. For $s=0$ the same
strict-local quotient is the zero ring and the branch lemma gives length zero for every
presentation. Formula, coordinate independence and etale invariance follow in all cases; for an
arbitrary etale pullback use the pulled-back atlas [lem-weil-pbc-etale-scheme-base-change].
Uses: lem-trf-nc-coordinate-max-stratum, def-trf-normal-crossings-branch-count,
lem-weil-pbc-etale-scheme-base-change, lem-trf-parameter-product-strict-local-branches,
lem-trf-ind-etale-strict-local-invariance.
6. Fix $k\ge1$. At $u$, the inequality $b_D(e(u))\ge k$ says exactly that some $k$-element
subset of $\{1,\ldots,s\}$ vanishes there. Thus $e^{-1}(T_k)=\bigcup_{|J|=k}V(t_j:j\in J)$, a
finite union of closed subsets, empty for $k>s$. In particular $W\setminus e^{-1}(T_k)$ is open.
Its image is open by [lem-weil-pbc-etale-morphism-open-map]. By Step 5 that image lies in
$X\setminus T_k$. Conversely every point outside $T_k$ lifts to one of our charts and lies in
that image. Hence $X\setminus T_k$ is the union of these open images, proving that $T_k$ is
closed.
Uses: lem-weil-pbc-etale-morphism-open-map.
7. The images $e(W)$ form an open covering of $X$ by [lem-weil-pbc-etale-morphism-open-map]. If
$X$ is quasi-compact, choose finitely many charts covering it. On the image of each chart Step 5
bounds the branch count by its finite selected-list length $s$. Their maximum bounds $b_D$ on
$X$. If $X$ is empty, use the bound zero. This also covers $B=\varnothing$, $D=\varnothing$,
zero-dimensional stalks and charts with no selected factors.
Uses: lem-weil-pbc-etale-morphism-open-map.
-/
theorem nc_subboundary_branch_atlas
    {X : Scheme.{u}} (_hX : IsRegularScheme X) [IsLocallyNoetherian X]
    (B D : X.IdealSheafData) (_hB : B.IsNormalCrossingsDivisor)
    (_hDB : B ≤ D) [IsReduced D.subscheme]
    (_hcomponents : IsUnionOfIrreducibleComponents D B)
    (_hneighborhood : ∀ (Ω : Type (max u v)) [Field Ω] [IsAlgClosed Ω]
      (x : X) (ι : X.residueField x →+* Ω),
      ∃ (V : Scheme.{u}) (e : V ⟶ X), Etale e ∧ IsRegularScheme V ∧ IsNoetherian V ∧
        ∃ (w : V) (he : e w = x) (ιV : V.residueField w →+* Ω),
          ιV.comp (e.residueFieldMap w).hom =
            ι.comp (X.residueFieldCongr he).hom.hom ∧
          ∀ z : V, ∃ (hR : IsNoetherianRing (V.presheaf.stalk z))
            (t a : List (V.presheaf.stalk z)),
            @IsLocalRing.IsRegularSystemOfParameters (V.presheaf.stalk z) _ _ hR t ∧
              a.Sublist t ∧ ∃ (U : V.affineOpens) (hz : z ∈ U.1),
                ((B.comap e).ideal U).map (V.presheaf.germ U.1 z hz).hom =
                  Ideal.span {a.prod}) :
    (∀ (Ω : Type (max u v)) [Field Ω] [IsAlgClosed Ω]
      (x : X) (ι : X.residueField x →+* Ω),
      ∃ (W : Scheme.{u}) (e : W ⟶ X),
        IsAffine W ∧ Etale e ∧ IsRegularScheme W ∧ IsNoetherian W ∧
        ∃ (w : W) (he : e w = x) (ιW : W.residueField w →+* Ω),
          ιW.comp (e.residueFieldMap w).hom =
            ι.comp (X.residueFieldCongr he).hom.hom ∧
          ∃ (d r s : ℕ) (hr : r ≤ d) (hs : s ≤ r) (t : Fin d → Γ(W, ⊤))
            (hR : IsNoetherianRing (W.presheaf.stalk w)),
            @IsLocalRing.IsRegularSystemOfParameters (W.presheaf.stalk w) _ _ hR
              (List.ofFn fun j ↦ W.presheaf.germ ⊤ w (by trivial) (t j)) ∧
            D.comap e = ofIdealTop
              (Ideal.span {∏ j : Fin s, t (Fin.castLE (hs.trans hr) j)}) ∧
            B.comap e = ofIdealTop (Ideal.span {∏ j : Fin r, t (Fin.castLE hr j)}) ∧
            (∀ j : Fin r, W.Γevaluation w (t (Fin.castLE hr j)) = 0) ∧
            (∀ z : W, ∃ (hZ : IsNoetherianRing (W.presheaf.stalk z))
              (q a : List (W.presheaf.stalk z)),
              @IsLocalRing.IsRegularSystemOfParameters (W.presheaf.stalk z) _ _ hZ q ∧
                a.Sublist q ∧
                (∀ j : Fin r,
                  W.Γevaluation z (t (Fin.castLE hr j)) = 0 ↔
                    W.presheaf.germ ⊤ z (by trivial) (t (Fin.castLE hr j)) ∈ a) ∧
                (∀ i j : Fin r,
                  W.Γevaluation z (t (Fin.castLE hr i)) = 0 →
                  W.Γevaluation z (t (Fin.castLE hr j)) = 0 → i ≠ j →
                  W.presheaf.germ ⊤ z (by trivial) (t (Fin.castLE hr i)) ≠
                    W.presheaf.germ ⊤ z (by trivial) (t (Fin.castLE hr j)))) ∧
            (∀ z : W, D.ncBranchCount (e z) =
              Nat.card {j : Fin s //
                W.Γevaluation z (t (Fin.castLE (hs.trans hr) j)) = 0}) ∧
            (∀ (k : ℤ), 1 ≤ k →
              e ⁻¹' {z : X | k ≤ (D.ncBranchCount z : ℤ)} =
                ⋃ (J : Finset (Fin s)) (_ : (J.card : ℤ) = k),
                  ((ofIdealTop (Ideal.span (Set.range fun j : {j // j ∈ J} ↦
                    t (Fin.castLE (hs.trans hr) j.1)))).support : Set W))) ∧
    (∀ (x : X) (n : ℕ), D.HasNCBranchChart x n → n = D.ncBranchCount x) ∧
    (∀ (W : Scheme.{u}) (e : W ⟶ X) [Etale e] (w : W),
      (D.comap e).ncBranchCount w = D.ncBranchCount (e w)) ∧
    (∀ (k : ℤ), 1 ≤ k → IsClosed {x : X | k ≤ (D.ncBranchCount x : ℤ)}) ∧
    (CompactSpace X → ∃ N : ℕ, ∀ x : X, D.ncBranchCount x ≤ N) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData
